Q. What is React?
A. React is a JavaScript library for building component-based user interfaces.
  Its major concepts are:
  Components
  Props
  State
  Hooks
  Virtual DOM
  Unidirectional data flow
  Reusable component architecture
  Redux/Redux Toolkit or React Query

Q. What is JSX?
A. JSX is a syntax extension that allows us to write HTML-like code inside JavaScript.
    const element = <h1>Hello {name}</h1>;
    JSX is transformed into JavaScript calls during compilation.
    Important: JSX is not HTML. It follows JavaScript/React rules, such as className instead of class.

Q. What is the Virtual DOM?
A. The Virtual DOM is an in-memory representation of the UI.
    When state or props change:
    React creates a new virtual representation.
    React compares it with the previous representation.
    It determines what changed.
    React updates the required parts of the real DOM.

    This comparison process is part of reconciliation.

Q. What is the difference between props and state in React? Explain default props in React?
A. Props are inputs passed from a parent component to a child and should be treated as read-only.
    State is data managed by the component itself and can change over time.

    Props → passed by parent
    State → managed by component

    default props are fallback values used when a component does not receive a particular prop.
    function User({ name = "Guest", age = 18 }) {
      return (
        <div>
          <h2>{name}</h2>
          <p>Age: {age}</p>
        </div>
      );
    }

  Props	                        State
  Passed from parent	          Managed by component
  Read-only	                    Can change
  Used to configure component	  Used for dynamic data
  Parent controls them	        Component controls them

Q. What is prop drilling?
A. Prop drilling occurs when data is passed through multiple intermediate components just to reach a deeply nested component.
  Even if Parent, Child, and GrandChild don't need the data, they have to pass it.
  Solutions include:
  Context API
  Redux/Zustand
  Component composition
  Custom hooks
  Server-state libraries where appropriate

Q. What is component composition?
A. Composition means building complex components by combining smaller components rather than creating a large inheritance hierarchy.
    Example:
    <Card>
      <UserProfile />
    </Card>    
    The Card component doesn't need to know what is inside it.
    Strong point: React generally favors composition over inheritance.

Q. What are controlled and uncontrolled components?
A. Controlled : React controls the input value.

  const [name, setName] = useState("");
  <input
    value={name}
    onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)}
  />

  Uncontrolled : The DOM maintains the value.

  const inputRef = useRef();
  <input ref={inputRef} />
  Controlled components are commonly preferred for complex forms because React has full control over the form state.

Q. What are React Hooks?
A. Hooks allow functional components to use React features such as state, context, refs, and effects.
  Common hooks:
    useState
    useEffect
    useContext
    useReducer
    useRef
    useMemo
    useCallback
    useLayoutEffect

  useState adds local state to a functional component.
    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
    setCount(count + 1);  // calling 

  React schedules a state update and may re-render the component.
  For updates based on previous state, I prefer the functional form:
    setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  This avoids relying on a potentially stale value.


  useEffect is used to synchronize a component with external systems such as:
    API calls
    subscriptions
    timers
    browser APIs
    event listeners

    useEffect(() => {
      fetchUsers();
    }, []);
  The empty dependency array means the effect doesn't depend on changing reactive values.
  Important interview point: 
  useEffect should not be treated as a general-purpose place for every piece of component logic. 
  If something can be calculated during rendering, an effect may be unnecessary.


  useMemo memoizes the result of an expensive calculation.
  const filteredUsers = useMemo(() => {
    return users.filter(user => user.active);
  }, [users]);
  The calculation is recomputed when dependencies change.
  Important: I don't use useMemo everywhere. It has its own overhead and should be used when memoization actually helps.


  useCallback memoizes a function reference.
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("clicked");
  }, []);
  It's particularly useful when passing callbacks to memoized child components or when function identity matters.

  useRef provides a mutable value that persists between renders without causing a re-render when it changes.
    Common uses:
    
    DOM references
    Timers
    Previous values
    Mutable instance-like values

    Example:
    const inputRef = useRef();
    inputRef.current.focus();

Q. What are the rules of Hooks?
A. Two major rules:
    Call Hooks only at the top level.
    Call Hooks only from React components or custom Hooks.

  useEffect(() => {
    if (isLoggedIn) {
      // logic
    }
  }, [isLoggedIn]);

Q. Difference between useMemo and useCallback.
A.   useMemo     → memoizes a calculated value
      useCallback → memoizes a function

Q. Difference between useState & useRef?
A.   useState	                            useRef
    Updating causes render	            Updating doesn't cause render
    Used for UI state	                  Used for mutable values/DOM references
    Value managed by React rendering	  current is mutable

Q. When should you NOT use useMemo or useCallback?
A. I don't use them by default.
  If the calculation is cheap or the child isn't sensitive to function identity, memoization can make the code more complex without providing meaningful benefit.
  I first identify an actual performance issue using profiling, then optimize.

Q. What is the dependency array in useEffect?
A. useEffect(() => {
    console.log(userId);
  }, [userId]);
  The effect runs when userId changes.

  Three common forms:
  useEffect(() => {});  // Runs after every relevant render.
  useEffect(() => {}, []);  // Runs after initial mount, subject to development Strict Mode behavior.
  useEffect(() => {}, [userId]);  // Runs when userId changes.

Q. What is cleanup in useEffect?
A. Cleanup is used to remove subscriptions, timers, listeners, etc.
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      console.log("Running");
    }, 1000);
  
    return () => {
      clearInterval(timer);
    };
  }, []);
  Without cleanup, we can create memory leaks or duplicate subscriptions.

Q. How do you cancel an API request in React?
A. I use AbortController
  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();  
    fetch("/api/users", {
      signal: controller.signal
    });  
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, []);
  This is especially useful when a component unmounts or the request becomes obsolete

Q. How do you handle API calls in React?
A. useEffect + fetch/axios
    For production applications with significant server state, I prefer a server-state solution such as TanStack Query.
    Typical requirements include:
    
    Loading state
    Error state
    Retry
    Cancellation
    Caching
    Pagination
    Refetching
    Authentication
    Error handling

Q. How do you prevent race conditions in API calls?
A. Suppose the user searches:

  React
  React developer
  React developer India
  
  Responses may arrive out of order. I can solve this using:
  
  AbortController
  Request IDs
  Query libraries
  Debouncing
  Ignoring stale responses
  
  For example, aborting the previous request when a new search begins prevents obsolete results from being applied.

Q. What is a stale closure?
A. A stale closure happens when a callback captures an old value from a previous render.
  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      console.log(count);
    }, 1000);
    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []);
  Here the callback can keep referring to the initial count.
  Possible solutions include:
    Correct dependencies
    Functional state updates
    useRef when appropriate

Q. What is React.memo?
A. React.memo prevents a functional component from re-rendering when its props are considered unchanged.
    const User = React.memo(({ name }) => {
      return <div>{name}</div>;
    });
  However, if an object/function is recreated every render, shallow comparison can still detect a different reference.
  That's where stable references from useMemo or useCallback may help.

Q. What causes a React component to re-render?
A. Common causes include:
    Its state changes
    Its parent re-renders
    Its context value changes
    Its subscribed external store changes
    Its props change
    A parent re-render doesn't necessarily mean the DOM will be changed. React still performs reconciliation.

Q. How do you optimize a slow React application?
A. I generally follow this process:
    Profile the application.
    Identify unnecessary renders.
    Split large components.
    Use React.memo where appropriate.
    Use useMemo/useCallback selectively.
    Virtualize large lists.
    Lazy-load large components.
    Optimize API calls.
    Cache server state.
    Reduce unnecessary context updates.
    Optimize images/assets.
    Analyze bundle size.
    
    I would avoid premature optimization.

Q. Context API vs Redux?
A. Context : Good for relatively stable/global values such as:
    Theme
    Locale
    Authentication information

  Redux : Useful when application state is complex and needs:
    Predictable updates
    Centralized state
    Middleware
    DevTools
    Complex cross-component workflows

I choose based on application requirements rather than automatically using Redux.

Q. What is Redux?
A. Redux is a predictable state management library. 
  Modern Redux applications generally use Redux Toolkit rather than writing Redux boilerplate manually.

    Typical flow:
    
    Component
       ↓
    dispatch(action)
       ↓
    Reducer
       ↓
    Store updated
       ↓
    Component receives updated state

  
  Advantages of Redux
  1. Centralized state management
  2. Predictable state updates
  3. Avoids prop drilling
  4. Easy debugging
  5. Predictable data flow
  6. Good for large applications
  7. State can be shared across unrelated components

  Don't say: "Redux is always better than useState/useContext."
  Redux is more valuable when you have complex global state, many components sharing state, 
  frequent state transitions, or a need for predictable/debuggable state management.

Q. What is Redux Toolkit?
A. Redux Toolkit is the recommended way to write Redux logic. It provides:
  configureStore
  createSlice
  createAsyncThunk
  Immer integration
  Simplified Redux configuration

  Example:
  const counterSlice = createSlice({
    name: "counter",
    initialState: { value: 0 },
    reducers: {
      increment: state => {
        state.value++;
      }
    }
  });

Q. Redux vs React Query?
A. They solve different problems. Redux is commonly used for client/application state.
  React Query/TanStack Query is designed primarily for server state:

  API caching
  Refetching
  Retry
  Loading/error states
  Cache invalidation
  Background updates
For a modern application, I wouldn't automatically put all API data into Redux.

Q. How do you implement lazy loading in React?
A. Using dynamic imports: const Dashboard = lazy(() => import("./Dashboard"));
    <Suspense fallback={<Loader />}>
      <Dashboard />
    </Suspense>

This allows the application to split the bundle and load the component when needed.

Q. What is Suspense?
A. Suspense allows React to display a fallback while a child is waiting for something that supports Suspense.
    A common use is lazy-loaded components:
    
    <Suspense fallback={<Loading />}>
      <Dashboard />
    </Suspense>
    
    It can also participate in other React-supported async rendering patterns.

Q. How do you handle large forms in React?
A. For large forms, I consider:

    Controlled vs uncontrolled inputs
    React Hook Form
    Schema validation with Zod/Yup
    Field-level validation
    Avoiding unnecessary re-renders
    Dynamic fields
    Server-side validation
    Error handling

  For a very large form, I generally avoid putting every field into a parent component's state if it causes unnecessary rendering.

Q. How do you prevent XSS in React?
A. React escapes values rendered normally:

  <div>{userInput}</div>
  
  The major risk is intentionally injecting raw HTML:
  
  dangerouslySetInnerHTML
  
  If raw HTML is required, I sanitize it using a trusted sanitizer and follow a strict content security policy.
  
  I never assume client-side validation is sufficient for security.

Q. What is reconciliation?
A. Reconciliation is React's process of comparing the previous rendered tree with the new one and determining the minimal changes needed for the UI.
  Keys are especially important because they help React identify elements across renders.

Q. What is Fiber?
A. Fiber is React's internal architecture for representing and processing work on the component tree.
  It enables React to:
  
  Break rendering work into units
  Prioritize work
  Pause/resume certain rendering work
  Support concurrent rendering capabilities
  
  For an experienced React developer, understanding Fiber conceptually is more important than knowing its internal implementation details.

Q. What is concurrent rendering?
A. Concurrent rendering allows React to work on rendering without necessarily blocking everything else until the entire update is complete.
  React can prioritize more important updates and defer less important work.
  
  For example, startTransition can mark an update as non-urgent.
  
  startTransition(() => {
    setSearchResults(results);
  });
  
  This can help keep interactions responsive

Q. useTransition lets us mark some state updates as non-urgent.
A. useTransition lets us mark some state updates as non-urgent.
    const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
    
    startTransition(() => {
      setSearchResults(data);
    });
    
    React can prioritize urgent interactions over the transition update.

Q .What is the difference between useEffect and useLayoutEffect?
A. useEffect runs after the browser has generally had a chance to paint.
  useLayoutEffect runs synchronously after DOM mutations but before the browser paints.  
  Use useLayoutEffect when you need to measure or synchronously adjust DOM layout to avoid visible flicker.
  
  Example:
  
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const width = elementRef.current.offsetWidth;
  }, []);
  
  For normal API calls and subscriptions, useEffect is usually preferable.

Q. What is a custom Hook?
A. A custom Hook is a reusable function whose name starts with use and which can use other Hooks.
  Example:
  
  function useDebounce(value, delay) {
    const [debouncedValue, setDebouncedValue] = useState(value);
    useEffect(() => {
      const timer = setTimeout(() => {
        setDebouncedValue(value);
      }, delay);
      return () => clearTimeout(timer);
    }, [value, delay]);
    return debouncedValue;
  }
  
  This allows reusable stateful logic without duplicating component code.

Q. What are Error Boundaries?
A. Error boundaries catch errors during rendering and certain lifecycle operations in their child component tree and show a fallback UI instead of crashing the entire UI tree.
Traditionally they are implemented using class components.

  Conceptually:
  
  Application
     ↓
  Error Boundary
     ↓
  Feature
  
  For production applications, I typically place boundaries around meaningful feature areas rather than relying on one giant boundary.

Q. How do you test React applications?
A. I generally use:

  Vitest/Jest for unit tests
  React Testing Library for component behavior
  Playwright/Cypress for end-to-end testing
  
  I focus tests on user behavior rather than implementation details.
  
  For example, instead of testing an internal state variable, I test:
  
  User clicks Login
         ↓
  Loading appears
         ↓
  API succeeds
         ↓
  Dashboard appears

Q. How would you structure a large React application?
A. For a large application, I prefer feature-oriented organization.

  For example:
  
  src/
   ├── app/
   ├── features/
   │    ├── auth/
   │    ├── users/
   │    ├── orders/
   │    └── dashboard/
   ├── components/
   ├── hooks/
   ├── services/
   ├── utils/
   ├── routes/
   └── types/
  
  I keep business logic close to the feature that owns it and avoid creating a giant global components or utils folder containing unrelated code.

Q. How would you improve the performance of a React page containing 10,000 records?
A. I would not render all 10,000 DOM nodes at once. I'd use:
  1. Virtualization : Only visible rows are rendered.
        Libraries such as: react-window, TanStack Virtual
  2. Pagination : Load a limited number of records.
  3. Memoization : Use React.memo for expensive row components when appropriate.
  4. Stable callbacks : Use useCallback where function identity is causing unnecessary renders.
  5. Server-side filtering/sorting : Don't load unnecessary data to the browser.
  6. Profiling : Use React DevTools Profiler to identify the actual bottleneck.

Q. A child component is re-rendering unnecessarily. How would you debug it?
A. First, I wouldn't immediately add useMemo.
  I'd:
  
  Use React DevTools Profiler.
  Identify why the child renders.
  Check whether parent renders.
  Check whether props changed.
  Check object/array references.
  Check callback references.
  Check context updates.
  Apply React.memo if appropriate.
  Stabilize props with useMemo/useCallback only where beneficial.
  Profile again.

  Example problem:
  
  <Child options={{ page: 1 }} />
  
  A new object is created on every parent render.
  
  Potential solution:
  
  const options = useMemo(() => ({
    page: 1
  }), []);


<Child options={options} />

But I'd only do this if profiling shows it matters.

